nd your code. This usually happens if the repository was initialized locally but a \"remote\" destination (like GitHub) hasn't been linked to it yet.\n\nTo fix this, follow these steps in your Cloud Shell terminal:\n\n### 1. Check current remotes\nRun this command to see if any remotes are already configured:\n```bash\ngit remote -v\n```\nIf the output is empty, you definitely need to add the remote.\n\n### 2. Add the 'origin' remote\nReplace `\u003cYOUR_REPO_URL\u003e` with your actual GitHub repository URL (e.g., `https://github.com/username/project.git` or `git@github.com:username/project.git`).\n\n**Using HTTPS (Easiest for Cloud Shell):**\n```bash\ngit remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/YOUR_REPO_NAME.git\n```\n\n**Using SSH (Requires SSH keys to be set up):**\n```bash\ngit remote add origin git@github.com:YOUR_USERNAME/YOUR_REPO_NAME.git\n```\n\n### 3. Push your branch again\nNow that the remote is linked, try pushing the test branch again:\n```bash\ngit push origin test-opa-ci\n```\n\n### Why did this happen?\nWhen you created the GitHub Action file `/home/mdbaddi/.github/workflows/opa-ci.yml`, it existed in your local directory. However, Git needs to know the specific server address associated with the name `origin` to synchronize the files. Once you run `git remote add`, the link is established permanently for this local folder.\n\n\u003c!--\n[PROMPT_SUGGESTION]How do I set up an SSH key in Google Cloud Shell for my GitHub account?[/PROMPT_SUGGESTION]\n[PROMPT_SUGGESTION]What should I do if git push asks for a username and password every time?[/PROMPT_SUGGESTION]\n--\u003e","chatSectionId":"4","ideContext":{"currentFile":{"path":"/home/mdbaddi/.github/workflows/opa-ci.yml","language":"yaml","selection":{"start":0,"end":0},"includedReason":"CURRENTLY_OPEN"},"remoteContexts":null,"remoteRepositories":[],"otherFiles":[{"path":"/home/mdbaddi/.local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/pip/_internal/vcs/git.py","language":"","selection":{"start":0,"end":