.\n\n### Next Steps to Finalize the Setup\n\n1.  **Create the Pull Request**: \n    Click the link provided in your terminal output: https://github.com/Manjuanthtek/odp-runtime/pull/new/test-opa-ci. \n    This will open the GitHub UI to create a Pull Request into your `main` branch.\n\n2.  **Verify the CI Workflow**:\n    Once the Pull Request is created, navigate to the **Actions** tab in your GitHub repository. You should see the **OPA CI/CD** workflow start automatically. It will run the `opa-validation` job, which performs:\n    *   Rego formatting checks (`opa fmt`)\n    *   Syntax validation (`opa check`)\n    *   Unit tests (`opa test`)\n\n3.  **Check for Large Files**:\n    Your logs show a warning about a large file: `.cache/cloud-code/cloudcode_cli/cloudcode_cli/d005c37d/cloudcode_cli` (99.81 MB). \n    GitHub has a recommended limit of 50 MB and a hard limit of 100 MB. Since this is a cache file, it is highly recommended to exclude it from your repository to keep the repo size manageable and avoid future push failures.\n\n### Recommended Improvement: .gitignore\n\nSince you are working in a Cloud Shell environment, you should add a `.gitignore` file to ensure IDE and tool caches don't get pushed to your OPA project.\n\n```diff\n--- /dev/null\n+++ /home/mdbaddi/.gitignore\n@@ -0,0 +1,7 @@\n+# Cloud Shell / Cloud Code caches\n+.cache/\n+\n+# OPA Bundles\n+bundle.tar.gz\n+\n+# OS metadata\n+.DS_Store\n```\n\n### Verification in GitHub\nAfter you merge the Pull Request into `main`, the **CD** portion of your workflow (`opa-deploy`) will trigger. This will:\n1.  Build your production OPA policy bundle.\n2.  Log in to the GitHub Container Registry.\n3.  Build and push a new Docker image for your OPA pod.\n\n\u003c!--\n[PROMPT_SUGGESTION]How do I check the logs of the GitHub Action if the OPA tests fail?[/PROMPT_SUGGESTION]\n[PROMPT_SUGGESTION]What should I add to my Dockerfile to properly serve the OPA bundle in a pod?[/PROMPT_SUGGESTION]\n--\u003e","chatSectio